==Old English==

===Etymology===
From Germanic, from Indo-European ''*egom'' ‘I’, from a presumed form ''*eg''.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian ''[[ik]]'', Old Saxon ''[[ik]]'' (Dutch ''[[ik]]''), Old High German ''[[ih]]'' (German ''[[ich]]''), Old Norse ''[[ek]]'' (Swedish ''[[jag]]''), Gothic '''[[𐌹𐌺]]'''.  The IE root, in various forms, is also the source of Sanskrit [[अहम्]], Latin ''[[ego]]'' (Spanish ''[[yo]]'' etc.), Ancient Greek '''{{polytonic|[[ἐγώ]]}}''', Lithuanian ''[[eo]]'', Avestan ''azəm'', Old Church Slavonic ''az’'' (Russian ''[[я]]''), Latvian ''[[es]]'', Armenian {{term|ես||sc=Armn|tr=es|lang=hy}}.  For declined derivations, see under ''[[me#Old English|mē]]'', ''[[we#Old English|wē]]'' etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/iʧ/}}

===Pronoun===
'''iċ'''

# [[I]], used by the speaker referring to himself or herself as the subject, or in agreement with that subject

